// rtc.js: the browser's real-time communication objects, for a peg.
//
// Part of the SDK, written by the Workpeg team: DEC-82 lists it among the
// SDK's own JavaScript files. A peg is Python only. The compiler turns a
// WebRTC call in a peg into a call on the fixed entries below, with the
// member's name taken from the compiler's own table, never from the peg's
// source text. This file looks the name up again, in its own table, and
// drives the browser's object, which the page never holds.
//
// What a page holds is a HANDLE: a frozen object whose prototype carries
// only getters, named in Python, and whose chain ends in null. There is no
// function on it that a peg could read and hand to something that calls it,
// and no toJSON, toString or valueOf. The browser object stays in REAL, a
// WeakMap inside this closure.
//
// Every value going in is copied into fresh plain data first, then checked,
// and only the checked copy reaches the browser. Most of it is not the
// peg's own: an offer, an answer and each candidate came from the other
// peer, through signaling.
//
// The media half: Workpeg.rtc.devices reaches navigator.mediaDevices, and
// what it captures (a camera, a microphone, a screen) comes back as a
// stream handle whose tracks are registered to the page at once, so the
// page's teardown turns them off. A URL camera is a <video> made here and
// never put on the page; its URL is https:, blob: or a path on the page's
// own origin.
//
// A data channel sends text or bytes and nothing else, and its binaryType
// is "arraybuffer" before the page holds it. Stats and a sender's
// parameters come back as fresh plain data, and set_parameters writes
// only the fields a peg may change over the browser's own, read afresh.
//
// An event reaches a method the page's class declares, by name, and only
// for an event its kind lists. The listener is this file's own; the method
// gets one fresh dict of data and handles, never the browser's event, and a
// message's data is text or a list of bytes. A close, or the page's
// teardown, takes the listeners away.
//
// Signaling is the Workpeg half beside the mapping: Workpeg.rtc.signaling's
// four members reach the api's people/, people/me/ and signals/ under the
// page's business, and nothing else, with their arguments checked first and
// their replies copied to the documented keys. On a loopback page that the
// fake-calls meta marks, they reach the page's own origin instead.
//
// Loaded after workpeg-runtime.js, same-origin, in the build's ?v= hash. It
// installs nothing unless the runtime is there, and a second load changes
// nothing. The entries, which are the compiler's contract:
//
//   Workpeg.rtc.make(owner, kind, args, kwargs)   -> a handle
//   Workpeg.rtc.call(owner, handle, member, args) -> data or handles, or for
//                                                    an awaited member always
//                                                    a Promise of them
//   Workpeg.rtc.on(owner, handle, event, method)
//   Workpeg.rtc.off(owner, handle, event, method)
//   Workpeg.rtc.set(handle, property, value)      -> None
//   Workpeg.rtc.devices, Workpeg.rtc.signaling      the two fixed handles
//   Workpeg._clearRtc(app)        the runtime's teardown, on navigation
//   Workpeg._rtcAttach(el, h)     the video and audio render
//
// A refusal is a realm Error carrying failure.problem, from the fixed lists
// in the table block. Nothing in this file writes markup, builds code from
// a string or starts a timer: tests/rtc/rtc_source.test.mjs reads it for
// each of those, comments included.
